\section{Introduction}

The field of artificial intelligence has experienced significant growth in recent years, with numerous applications in areas such as computer vision, natural language processing, and robotics \cite{LeCun2015}. A key aspect of this growth is the development of methods for solving complex problems, which often involve large amounts of data and require efficient processing \cite{Jordan2015}. In this context, the problem of optimizing machine learning models has become increasingly important, as it enables the improvement of model performance and the reduction of computational costs \cite{Bengio2012}. Existing methods for optimizing machine learning models can be broadly categorized into three research lines: gradient-based methods, evolutionary algorithms, and reinforcement learning \cite{Sutton2018}. Gradient-based methods, such as stochastic gradient descent, are widely used due to their simplicity and efficiency \cite{Bottou2010}. Evolutionary algorithms, including genetic algorithms and particle swarm optimization, have also been applied to optimize machine learning models \cite{Eiben2015}. Reinforcement learning, which involves training agents to make decisions in complex environments, has been used to optimize model hyperparameters \cite{Sutton2018}.

These existing methods have achieved significant success in various applications; however, they also have limitations. For example, gradient-based methods can converge to local optima, while evolutionary algorithms can be computationally expensive \cite{Bengio2012}. Reinforcement learning methods can be challenging to implement and require careful tuning of hyperparameters \cite{Sutton2018}. Furthermore, existing methods often focus on optimizing a single objective, such as model accuracy or computational cost, without considering the trade-offs between different objectives \cite{Liu2019}. The relationship between these research lines is complex, with each line having its strengths and weaknesses. For instance, gradient-based methods are often used in conjunction with evolutionary algorithms to optimize model hyperparameters \cite{Eiben2015}. Reinforcement learning methods can be used to optimize the optimization process itself, by learning to adapt the optimization algorithm to the specific problem at hand \cite{Sutton2018}.

The limitations of existing methods and the complexity of the optimization problem motivate the development of new approaches. A key challenge is to develop methods that can efficiently optimize machine learning models while considering multiple objectives and constraints \cite{Liu2019}. Another challenge is to develop methods that can adapt to changing problem conditions and learn from experience \cite{Sutton2018}. The research question of how to optimize machine learning models in a way that balances competing objectives and adapts to changing conditions is a crucial one. This question is important because it has significant implications for the development of efficient and effective machine learning systems. By addressing this question, we can develop methods that are more robust, more efficient, and more effective in real-world applications. The technical opportunity to develop such methods is significant, as it can enable the creation of more sophisticated and autonomous machine learning systems \cite{LeCun2015}.

The proposed approach aims to address the identified challenges by developing a novel optimization method that combines elements of gradient-based methods, evolutionary algorithms, and reinforcement learning. This method will use a hybrid approach to optimize machine learning models, leveraging the strengths of each research line to overcome the limitations of existing methods. The proposed method will be designed to adapt to changing problem conditions and learn from experience, enabling it to optimize models more efficiently and effectively. The connection to the research motivation is clear, as the proposed method aims to address the key challenges and research questions identified in the motivation section. By developing a novel optimization method that combines multiple research lines, we can create a more robust and efficient optimization process that balances competing objectives and adapts to changing conditions.

The proposed method will be introduced in a way that is easy to understand, without relying on technical details. The key idea is to develop a method that can optimize machine learning models in a way that is both efficient and effective. This will be achieved by combining the strengths of different research lines and leveraging the latest advances in machine learning and optimization. The proposed method will be designed to be flexible and adaptable, enabling it to be applied to a wide range of problems and applications. The connection to the identified challenges is clear, as the proposed method aims to address the key challenges and research questions identified in the motivation section. By developing a novel optimization method that combines multiple research lines, we can create a more robust and efficient optimization process that balances competing objectives and adapts to changing conditions. The proposed method will be described in more detail in the following sections, including the key components and the way it addresses the identified challenges.

The contributions of this work are:
\begin{itemize}
\item A novel optimization method that combines elements of gradient-based methods, evolutionary algorithms, and reinforcement learning to optimize machine learning models.
\item A hybrid approach that leverages the strengths of each research line to overcome the limitations of existing methods and balance competing objectives.
\item An adaptive optimization process that learns from experience and adapts to changing problem conditions, enabling more efficient and effective optimization.
\item An experimental validation of the proposed method on a range of benchmark problems, demonstrating its effectiveness and efficiency in optimizing machine learning models.
\end{itemize}